WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:04.760
As Game of Thrones moved towards its epic
conclusion, almost every action shed new light

00:00:04.760 --> 00:00:06.620
on something that had been said before.

00:00:06.620 --> 00:00:11.020
From cryptic prophecies to long-awaited callbacks,
here are the lines that deserve a little extra

00:00:11.020 --> 00:00:14.860
attention — even the shortest sentences
might have more meaning than you think.

00:00:15.220 --> 00:00:19.700
Bran's transition from young, naive boy to
the Three-Eyed Raven and ruler of the Kingdoms

00:00:19.700 --> 00:00:23.660
of Westeros was a plotline that ran through
the entire series, from the first episode

00:00:23.660 --> 00:00:24.660
to the last.

00:00:24.740 --> 00:00:28.580
As he became more and more omniscient, he
essentially became a totally different person

00:00:28.580 --> 00:00:30.680
— or non-person, as the case may be.

00:00:30.680 --> 00:00:34.520
As each of the Stark siblings reunites with
Bran, they gradually figure out that he's

00:00:34.520 --> 00:00:36.380
turned a bit… well, odd.

00:00:36.380 --> 00:00:40.780
Neither Sansa nor Arya seem to be able to
make any sense out of his proclamation that

00:00:40.780 --> 00:00:44.320
he's the Three-Eyed Raven, although they do
use his talents to hold Littlefinger accountable

00:00:44.320 --> 00:00:45.320
for his crimes.

00:00:45.380 --> 00:00:49.720
In the season eight premiere, Jon Snow finally
meets back up with his long lost little brother,

00:00:49.740 --> 00:00:52.480
and sets up one of Bran's most cryptic lines
of dialogue:

00:00:53.220 --> 00:00:54.920
"Look at you.

00:00:56.140 --> 00:00:57.380
You're a man."

00:00:58.240 --> 00:00:59.240
"Almost."

00:00:59.500 --> 00:01:03.300
In the context of the final episode, Bran's
reply here takes on an entirely different

00:01:03.300 --> 00:01:04.300
meaning.

00:01:04.320 --> 00:01:08.300
Not only does it indicate that he's not quite
a regular man as the Three-Eyed Raven, the

00:01:08.320 --> 00:01:11.640
"almost" hints that he still has a major piece
of his destiny to fulfill: being named as

00:01:11.640 --> 00:01:12.640
the new king.

00:01:13.300 --> 00:01:15.300
"Why do you think I came all this way?"

00:01:16.020 --> 00:01:19.900
It's not surprising that Jaime Lannister's
return to Winterfell brings with it some fairly

00:01:19.909 --> 00:01:24.149
awkward reunions, considering that the last
time he came to visit to the Northern castle,

00:01:24.149 --> 00:01:27.080
he tried to kill a child by shoving the poor
kid out of a window.

00:01:27.080 --> 00:01:30.899
As we're all well aware, though, Jaime the
Kingslayer has plenty of other sins under

00:01:30.900 --> 00:01:34.160
his belt that go beyond his long-running affair
with his own sister.

00:01:34.160 --> 00:01:37.999
Of course, nobody besides Bran knows that
Jaime is the man who tried to kill him all

00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:39.000
those years ago.

00:01:39.220 --> 00:01:40.500
"The things I do for love."

00:01:43.280 --> 00:01:47.499
As Jaime stands in front of both the Stark
and Targaryen factions at Winterfell, ready

00:01:47.500 --> 00:01:50.700
to answer for his crimes, he comes face to
face with Bran himself.

00:01:50.700 --> 00:01:54.800
He seems to be steeling himself for the moment
where Bran reveals one of Jaime's worst moments,

00:01:54.800 --> 00:01:56.540
but that revelation never comes.

00:01:56.560 --> 00:02:00.600
Instead, Bran simply echoes Jaime's exact
words from the moment where Jaime pushed him

00:02:00.600 --> 00:02:01.700
out of that tower.

00:02:02.480 --> 00:02:04.900
"The things we do for love."

00:02:09.440 --> 00:02:13.520
This ultimately lets Jaime know that despite
Bran's long journey to become the Three-Eyed

00:02:13.530 --> 00:02:18.000
Raven, a being who seems beyond concepts like
revenge and forgiveness, he'll never forget

00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:19.500
what Jaime once did to him.

00:02:19.940 --> 00:02:23.640
One of the season's most heartbreaking moments
comes right after the assault on Daenerys

00:02:23.640 --> 00:02:24.640
and her fleet.

00:02:24.640 --> 00:02:28.780
After losing one of her dragons, Rhaegal,
to one of Qyburn's scorpion spear launchers,

00:02:28.780 --> 00:02:30.840
her ships are attacked by Euron Greyjoy.

00:02:30.840 --> 00:02:35.220
In the skirmish, Missandei, who has long been
one of Daenerys' most trusted advisors, is

00:02:35.220 --> 00:02:37.060
kidnapped by Greyjoy soldiers.

00:02:37.060 --> 00:02:41.700
Tragically, Missandei ends up losing her life
so Cersei can prove a twisted point to Daenerys.

00:02:41.700 --> 00:02:46.020
Offered the opportunity to speak her last
words, Missandei gives her final response.

00:02:46.400 --> 00:02:48.340
"Dracarys."

00:02:48.880 --> 00:02:52.660
Fans of Thrones will recognize this as the
command Daenerys uses to make her dragons

00:02:52.660 --> 00:02:56.700
breathe fire on her enemies, but it means
even more to Missandei: the first time she

00:02:56.700 --> 00:03:00.550
ever saw Daenerys use it, the Mother of Dragons
employed it against a slave owner in who once

00:03:00.550 --> 00:03:03.360
owned and abused Missandei, which ultimately
freed her.

00:03:03.420 --> 00:03:04.600
“Dracarys."

00:03:05.020 --> 00:03:09.440
Even though Missandei can't directly unleash
dragonfire with her last words, it's one last

00:03:09.440 --> 00:03:13.290
reminder of how much Daenerys has given her
and how much power and love Daenerys inspires

00:03:13.290 --> 00:03:17.120
in her subjects — as well as the burning
rage Cersei has now unleashed.

00:03:18.400 --> 00:03:22.560
Jaime and Cersei Lannister have been inextricably
linked, often physically, since the very beginning

00:03:22.560 --> 00:03:23.560
of the show.

00:03:23.600 --> 00:03:27.760
Even after the show's seventh season, when
Jaime left his sister behind after she threatened

00:03:27.760 --> 00:03:31.200
to kill him, viewers knew that their destinies
were still intertwined, and the only question

00:03:31.200 --> 00:03:32.200
was how.

00:03:32.200 --> 00:03:35.800
Still, it might've been a little surprising
when, after years of character development

00:03:35.800 --> 00:03:39.960
and a slow-burn romance, Jaime left Brienne
and his Northern allies behind to rejoin his

00:03:39.960 --> 00:03:41.420
twin-slash-lover.

00:03:41.460 --> 00:03:45.260
After a fight to the death with Euron, he
still returns to his true love and queen in

00:03:45.260 --> 00:03:46.720
a last-ditch attempt to escape.

00:03:46.720 --> 00:03:51.010
When the two find their path entirely blocked
as the ceiling begins to cave in above them,

00:03:51.010 --> 00:03:53.460
Jaime says something that explains all of
his actions:

00:03:54.220 --> 00:03:56.520
"Nothing else matters.

00:03:58.160 --> 00:04:00.460
Nothing else matters.

00:04:00.460 --> 00:04:02.340
Only us."

00:04:02.650 --> 00:04:06.720
Since the beginning, these two have stuck
together, and as Jaime told Brienne back in

00:04:06.720 --> 00:04:09.990
Winterfell, almost every evil act he committed
was in service to her.

00:04:09.990 --> 00:04:13.160
For Jaime, a few years of playing the role
of a good guy just aren't enough to erase

00:04:13.160 --> 00:04:14.160
those sins.

00:04:14.180 --> 00:04:18.660
To him, the only thing real is his relationship
with Cersei, and they deserve to die together.

00:04:18.960 --> 00:04:23.160
After her victory at King's Landing, Daenerys
disappears for a few victory laps on Drogon

00:04:23.160 --> 00:04:25.940
the Dragon before returning to her newly conquered
city.

00:04:25.940 --> 00:04:30.540
Landing at the ruins of the Red Keep, Daenerys
addresses her Unsullied and Dothraki army,

00:04:30.540 --> 00:04:34.080
giving off distinctly tyrannical vibes as
she informs her troops that their war is far

00:04:34.080 --> 00:04:35.080
from over..

00:04:35.100 --> 00:04:38.620
It's tempting to think that Daenerys would
be satisfied in her quest for world domination

00:04:38.639 --> 00:04:42.000
now that she has killed her rival queen and
taken the capital city.

00:04:42.000 --> 00:04:45.220
She's even giving this speech while standing
above Cersei's body, buried in the rubble

00:04:45.220 --> 00:04:46.220
beneath her.

00:04:46.220 --> 00:04:50.820
Instead, she asks her armies to join her in
"liberating" every person in Westeros.

00:04:51.100 --> 00:04:54.680
"Will you break the wheel with me?"

00:04:54.820 --> 00:04:58.480
The speech is made to an overwhelming roar,
but at this point, the only wheel left to

00:04:58.500 --> 00:05:01.880
break is the one of conquest that Dany's going
to roll across the continent.

00:05:01.880 --> 00:05:06.000
Daenerys has always pledged to rid the world
of injustice, but in her quest for total control

00:05:06.009 --> 00:05:10.319
of every inch of the Seven Kingdoms, it becomes
frighteningly apparent that she's more power-hungry

00:05:10.320 --> 00:05:11.920
than anyone anticipated.

00:05:13.080 --> 00:05:16.819
After resigning from his position as Hand
of the Queen due to Daenerys "slaughtering

00:05:16.819 --> 00:05:21.379
a city" that had already surrendered, Tyrion
is taken into captivity to await his fate,

00:05:21.380 --> 00:05:24.949
which will most likely come in the form of
being roasted by dragon fire, just like his

00:05:24.949 --> 00:05:25.800
pal Varys.

00:05:25.800 --> 00:05:30.259
While in captivity, Tyrion is visited by Jon
Snow, who is equally troubled by Daenerys'

00:05:30.260 --> 00:05:33.440
decision to kill thousands of innocents in
King's Landing.

00:05:33.440 --> 00:05:38.259
Jon initially insists he's still firmly on
her side, but over the course of the conversation,

00:05:38.260 --> 00:05:39.920
Tyrion's bluntness seems to get through.

00:05:39.920 --> 00:05:43.659
There's an especially crucial moment when
Tyrion offers up a reminder that it's not

00:05:43.659 --> 00:05:47.620
just Jon's life that's in danger, since he's
the actual true heir, but that his rebellious

00:05:47.620 --> 00:05:51.600
sister Sansa probably won't last long against
the conquering dragon queen, either.

00:05:51.600 --> 00:05:55.560
It was all well and good when Daenerys was
destroying slavers, captors, and other evil

00:05:55.569 --> 00:05:59.539
men, but now she's hell-bent on destroying
anybody in her path, whether or not they truly

00:05:59.539 --> 00:06:00.539
deserve to die.

00:06:00.540 --> 00:06:04.680
When Tyrion tells Jon that love often overcomes
reason, Jon offers up a memorable proverb

00:06:04.680 --> 00:06:05.680
in response.

00:06:05.720 --> 00:06:07.660
"Love is
the death of duty."

00:06:13.520 --> 00:06:14.960
"You just came up with that?"

00:06:15.100 --> 00:06:19.319
The line actually came from Maester Aemon
Targaryen, with whom Jon spent time during

00:06:19.319 --> 00:06:21.620
his first stint with the Night's Watch at
Castle Black.

00:06:21.620 --> 00:06:24.320
Tyrion, however, has an equally sage response:

00:06:24.500 --> 00:06:28.800
"Sometimes duty is the death of love."

00:06:28.980 --> 00:06:33.699
With that line, Tyrion leads Jon to his inevitable
fate, and the realization that Jon must kill

00:06:33.700 --> 00:06:35.360
Daenerys to protect Westeros.

00:06:35.360 --> 00:06:39.740
With that, Jon leaves to give Dany one last
chance to step back from the edge of world-conquering

00:06:39.740 --> 00:06:42.800
madness, and when she doesn't, he does his
duty at last.

00:06:43.120 --> 00:06:47.600
In a pretty great twist of fate, all three
surviving Stark children end up right where

00:06:47.600 --> 00:06:50.100
it seems like they wanted to be back during
the first episode.

00:06:50.100 --> 00:06:54.330
Sansa is the beautiful queen that she always
fantasized about becoming, Jon is off to join

00:06:54.330 --> 00:06:58.200
the Night's Watch (again), and Arya has become
the skilled, fearsome adventurer she always

00:06:58.200 --> 00:06:59.340
wanted to be.

00:06:59.340 --> 00:07:02.960
During the tearful goodbye between them, Jon
even insists that Arya's welcome to come visit

00:07:02.960 --> 00:07:03.960
him up North.

00:07:04.020 --> 00:07:07.600
Castle Black might technically forbid women
from entering its gates, but Jon is pretty

00:07:07.620 --> 00:07:10.440
realistic about the fact that nobody would
be able to stop her.

00:07:10.440 --> 00:07:13.740
However, the younger of the Stark women tells
him she won't be able to visit.

00:07:13.740 --> 00:07:14.640
She has other plans.

00:07:14.840 --> 00:07:16.860
"I'm not going back north."

00:07:17.960 --> 00:07:19.520
“Where are you going?”

00:07:21.440 --> 00:07:23.140
"What's west of Westeros?"

00:07:23.380 --> 00:07:26.840
It's unsurprising that Arya is already planning
her escape from Westeros.

00:07:26.840 --> 00:07:30.940
Ever since her time exploring Braavos, she's
been hard to pin down, and now that the Realm

00:07:30.940 --> 00:07:34.160
is safe in the hands of her brother, it's
about time for her next adventure.

00:07:34.160 --> 00:07:36.680
There's a whole unknown world out there for
her to explore.

00:07:36.680 --> 00:07:40.120
After saying her farewells to her sister and
brothers, that's where she heads.

00:07:40.120 --> 00:07:44.219
In the episode's closing shots, viewers see
Arya one last time, gathering her weapons

00:07:44.219 --> 00:07:48.120
and arranging maps before proudly strolling
along a grand ship, which turns out to be

00:07:48.120 --> 00:07:49.880
adorned with the symbols of House Stark.

00:07:49.880 --> 00:07:52.660
No matter where she goes, she'll always have
Winterfell with her.

00:07:53.660 --> 00:07:58.480
Samwell Tarly was never a great fighter, but
since he managed to defy all the odds and

00:07:58.490 --> 00:08:02.199
fan expectations to survive to the final episode
of the series, it was clear that he would

00:08:02.200 --> 00:08:05.040
have a huge role to play during the ending
of Game of Thrones.

00:08:05.040 --> 00:08:07.349
A bookish and somewhat sheepish figure, Sam's
strength was always in his studies, from the

00:08:07.349 --> 00:08:11.180
time he did enough research on greyscale to
cure Jorah Mormont's affliction to the time

00:08:11.180 --> 00:08:14.420
he helped Bran confirm Jon's true heritage
as Aegon Targaryen.

00:08:14.420 --> 00:08:18.559
This all comes to a head during the series
finale, when Sam, dressed in the white robes

00:08:18.559 --> 00:08:22.559
of a Maester and a part of Tyrion's Small
Council, presents the Hand of the King with

00:08:22.560 --> 00:08:26.680
an enormous book written by the Archmaester,
with whom Sam worked with at the Citadel.

00:08:26.680 --> 00:08:30.620
Confirming a long-held fan theory that Sam
would write or at least help write the history

00:08:30.629 --> 00:08:34.410
of Westeros since Robert's Rebellion, Sam
reveals that the name of the tome is A Song

00:08:34.410 --> 00:08:35.520
of Ice and Fire.

00:08:35.780 --> 00:08:37.660
"I helped him with the title."

00:08:37.920 --> 00:08:42.360
This is, of course, the same title that author
George R.R. Martin gave to the series of books

00:08:42.370 --> 00:08:43.789
on which Game of Thrones was based.

00:08:43.789 --> 00:08:47.880
As a survivor of all eight seasons, Sam was
present for many of the major events of the

00:08:47.880 --> 00:08:51.660
series, so if he was meant to play the role
of a stand-in for Martin, that seems like

00:08:51.660 --> 00:08:53.680
a perfectly fitting end for his character.

00:08:53.680 --> 00:08:57.880
Maybe he'll be the one to finally finish writing
The Winds of Winter and A Dream of Spring.

00:08:58.300 --> 00:09:02.280
With all of our usual suspects for who would
sit on the Iron Throne either dead or imprisoned

00:09:02.290 --> 00:09:06.530
and the throne itself melted into slag, the
time comes for the remaining cast members

00:09:06.530 --> 00:09:08.440
to choose a new leader of Westeros.

00:09:08.440 --> 00:09:12.000
That's when Tyrion is brought before a new
council led by the last remaining members

00:09:12.010 --> 00:09:16.621
of the Seven Kingdoms' great houses, including
Yara Greyjoy, the entire Stark family, an

00:09:16.621 --> 00:09:21.460
unnamed Dornish prince, and long-lost faces
like Robyn Arryn and Edmure Tully, who gets

00:09:21.460 --> 00:09:24.220
dunked on so viciously that he might never
recover.

00:09:24.500 --> 00:09:26.000
"Uncle?

00:09:28.640 --> 00:09:29.680
Please sit."

00:09:30.100 --> 00:09:34.560
Tyrion is nominally there to stand trial for
betraying the queen, so it seems unlikely

00:09:34.580 --> 00:09:37.660
that he'd get the chance to suggest the next
ruler of Westeros himself.

00:09:37.660 --> 00:09:39.980
As usual, however, he's full of surprises.

00:09:39.980 --> 00:09:43.620
Asked if he would like to rule, he says that
he doesn't want it, but having had some time

00:09:43.630 --> 00:09:47.780
to mull it over, he's made up his mind about
the best candidate, and he reached his conclusion

00:09:47.780 --> 00:09:49.400
for a pretty surprising reason.

00:09:49.400 --> 00:09:53.040
He tells the assembled nobles that stories
are the most powerful things in the world,

00:09:53.040 --> 00:09:54.540
and then makes his endorsement.

00:09:55.420 --> 00:10:00.340
"Who has a better story than Bran the Broken?"

00:10:01.560 --> 00:10:05.620
By suggesting Bran Stark as the next King
of Westeros, as well as suggesting that all

00:10:05.631 --> 00:10:10.200
future rulers should be chosen by a council
rather than simply born into their title,

00:10:10.200 --> 00:10:14.770
Tyrion finally breaks the wheel Daenerys fought
so hard to destroy, introducing a more merit-based

00:10:14.770 --> 00:10:17.000
system of monarchy into the Seven Kingdoms.

00:10:17.220 --> 00:10:18.720
"You've had a strange journey."

00:10:19.340 --> 00:10:20.700
"Stranger than most."

00:10:20.860 --> 00:10:24.760
Perhaps surprisingly, everyone assembled agrees,
deciding that Bran does in fact have the best

00:10:24.760 --> 00:10:25.760
story.

00:10:25.800 --> 00:10:29.700
Certainly better than Jon, a secret king who
literally came back from the dead to ride

00:10:29.710 --> 00:10:33.900
a dragon into battle against an army of zombies
and tragically kill his aunt-slash-lover,

00:10:33.900 --> 00:10:38.850
or Arya, who became a faceless assassin, got
revenge for her father's death by taking out

00:10:38.850 --> 00:10:43.380
an entire noble house, and saved the entire
world by straight up shanking the Night King.

00:10:43.380 --> 00:10:47.360
Maybe Westeros has a different idea of what
makes for a good story than we do.

00:10:47.640 --> 00:10:49.900
